# A 3D scene in a notebook

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:cauldron_3d_kino, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:cauldron_3d_formats, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "../../cauldron_3d_formats")}
])
```

## What this notebook shows

`Cauldron3D.Kino` is a 3D scene in a cell. Elixir keeps the scene: meshes, materials,
lights and the camera. The browser showing the notebook draws it with three.js on its
own graphics card. The machine running the notebook draws nothing; it only sends the
changes.

The scene is described with `cauldron_gpu`'s structs, the same ones a game sends to
the native renderer, so anything built here also runs in a window or a terminal.

## An empty view

A view starts empty. Evaluating it shows the cell, black until something is in it.

```elixir
alias Cauldron3D.Scene.{Camera, Environment, Instance, Light, Material, Mesh, Quat, Texture, Transform}

view = Cauldron3D.Kino.new(height: 420)
```

## Things to draw

Meshes, textures and materials are uploaded once and answered with handles. Instances
place a mesh with its materials in the scene.

```elixir
checker = Cauldron3D.Kino.texture(view, Texture.checker(256, 8, {200, 200, 190, 255}, {70, 75, 80, 255}))

assets = %{
  plane: Cauldron3D.Kino.mesh(view, Mesh.plane(20, 5)),
  cube: Cauldron3D.Kino.mesh(view, Mesh.cube(1.0)),
  sphere: Cauldron3D.Kino.mesh(view, Mesh.sphere(0.8, 32)),
  floor: Cauldron3D.Kino.material(view, %Material{base_texture: checker, roughness: 0.4}),
  gold: Cauldron3D.Kino.material(view, %Material{base_color: {1.0, 0.78, 0.34, 1.0}, metallic: 1.0, roughness: 0.25}),
  plastic: Cauldron3D.Kino.material(view, %Material{base_color: {0.1, 0.35, 0.9, 1.0}, roughness: 0.35}),
  lamp: Cauldron3D.Kino.material(view, %Material{base_color: {1.0, 0.6, 0.2, 1.0}, unlit: true})
}
```

## The scene

One `submit/2` is one tick: everything in the list changes at once. The view above
updates as soon as this cell runs.

```elixir
Cauldron3D.Kino.submit(view, [
  %Environment{ambient_intensity: 0.25, exposure: 1.1, shadow_distance: 20.0},
  {:instance, 1, %Instance{mesh: assets.plane, materials: [assets.floor]}},
  {:instance, 2, %Instance{mesh: assets.sphere, materials: [assets.gold], transform: Transform.new(position: {-1.6, 0.8, 0.0})}},
  {:instance, 3, %Instance{mesh: assets.cube, materials: [assets.plastic], transform: Transform.new(position: {1.6, 0.5, 0.0})}},
  {:instance, 4, %Instance{mesh: assets.sphere, materials: [assets.lamp], shadows: false, transform: Transform.new(position: {0.0, 2.6, -2.0}, scale: 0.12)}},
  {:light, 1, %Light{kind: :directional, direction: {-0.5, -1.0, -0.35}, color: {1.0, 0.95, 0.85}, intensity: 3.0, shadows: true}},
  {:light, 2, %Light{kind: :point, position: {0.0, 2.6, -2.0}, color: {1.0, 0.6, 0.2}, intensity: 20.0, range: 8.0}},
  %Camera{position: {0.0, 2.0, 6.0}, rotation: Quat.yaw_pitch(0.0, -0.25), fov_y: 1.0}
])
```

## Moving things

`Kino.listen/2` runs a function every 50 milliseconds in the background. Each run sends
the cube's new turn as one tick; the browser blends between ticks, so it turns smoothly
at the display's rate.

```elixir
Kino.listen(50, fn tick ->
  Cauldron3D.Kino.submit(view, [
    {:instance, 3,
     %Instance{
       mesh: assets.cube,
       materials: [assets.plastic],
       transform: Transform.new(position: {1.6, 0.5, 0.0}, rotation: Quat.axis_angle({0.3, 1.0, 0.2}, tick * 0.05))
     }}
  ])
end)
```

## Looking around

Input on the cell comes back as the events a native window sends. Drag with the left
button to turn the camera around the middle of the scene, and use the wheel to come
closer or move away.

```elixir
defmodule Orbit do
  defstruct yaw: 0.0, pitch: -0.25, distance: 6.3, dragging: false

  def handle(orbit, {:mouse_button, :down, 1, _x, _y}), do: %{orbit | dragging: true}
  def handle(orbit, {:mouse_button, :up, 1, _x, _y}), do: %{orbit | dragging: false}

  def handle(%{dragging: true} = orbit, {:mouse_motion, _x, _y, dx, dy}),
    do: %{orbit | yaw: orbit.yaw - dx * 0.01, pitch: clamp(orbit.pitch - dy * 0.01, -1.4, -0.05)}

  def handle(orbit, {:mouse_wheel, _dx, dy}),
    do: %{orbit | distance: clamp(orbit.distance - dy, 2.0, 15.0)}

  def handle(orbit, _event), do: orbit

  def camera(orbit) do
    rotation = Quat.yaw_pitch(orbit.yaw, orbit.pitch)
    {x, y, z} = Quat.rotate(rotation, {0.0, 0.0, orbit.distance})
    %Camera{position: {x, y + 0.5, z}, rotation: rotation, fov_y: 1.0}
  end

  defp clamp(value, low, high), do: value |> max(low) |> min(high)
end
```

`Kino.listen/3` reads the view's events in the background, keeping the orbit between
them, and sends a new camera when it moves.

```elixir
view
|> Cauldron3D.Kino.events()
|> Kino.listen(%Orbit{}, fn {_client, event}, orbit ->
  moved = Orbit.handle(orbit, event)
  if moved != orbit, do: Cauldron3D.Kino.submit(view, [Orbit.camera(moved)])
  {:cont, moved}
end)
```

## A detailed scene

The courtyard example (`examples/courtyard`) has two downloaded scenes: a night-time
alley built from Poly Haven's CC0 models, and Intel's Sponza atrium (CC-BY). Download
them once from that folder:

```sh
mix courtyard.fetch alley
mix courtyard.fetch atrium
```

The scenes are plain Elixir modules written against `CauldronGPU.Target`, so they upload
to a view as they do to the native renderer. This cell loads them from the example.
`cauldron_3d_formats`, installed above, reads their glTF files.

```elixir
courtyard = Path.join(__DIR__, "../../examples/courtyard/lib/courtyard")

~w(scene.ex assets.ex kit.ex grounds.ex scenes.ex scenes/courtyard.ex scenes/alley/buildings.ex scenes/alley.ex scenes/atrium.ex)
|> Enum.map(&Path.join(courtyard, &1))
|> Kernel.ParallelCompiler.require(return_diagnostics: true)

show = fn view, scene, view_state ->
  pose = scene.start()
  {x, y, z} = pose.position

  camera = %Camera{
    position: {x, y + 1.7, z},
    rotation: Quat.yaw_pitch(pose.yaw, pose.pitch),
    fov_y: 1.1
  }

  with {:ok, placed} <- scene.upload(view, side: 1024) do
    Cauldron3D.Kino.submit(view, placed.changes ++ scene.frame(placed, view_state) ++ [camera])
    {:ok, placed}
  end
end
```

The alley: lamps, security lights and a fire in a barrel stove. Its textures and meshes
are about 80 MB, sent to the browser once.

```elixir
alley = Cauldron3D.Kino.new(height: 480)
{:ok, alley_placed} = show.(alley, Courtyard.Scenes.Alley, %{time: 0.0})
alley
```

The stove's fire flickers: `frame/2` gives its light for a moment in time.

```elixir
Kino.listen(50, fn tick ->
  Cauldron3D.Kino.submit(alley, Courtyard.Scenes.Alley.frame(alley_placed, %{time: tick * 0.05}))
end)
```

The atrium at three in the afternoon, with its textures at 1024 pixels a side. It is
about 3.7 million triangles and 170 MB to send, so it takes a while to appear.

```elixir
atrium = Cauldron3D.Kino.new(height: 480)
{:ok, _atrium_placed} = show.(atrium, Courtyard.Scenes.Atrium, %{hour: 15.0})
atrium
```

## The same scene elsewhere

A view is a `CauldronGPU.Target`, as a native renderer (`CauldronGPU.open/1`) and a
browser page (`Cauldron3D.Net.Scene`) are. Code written against `CauldronGPU.Target`
draws into any of them, and the `cauldron_gpu` converters upload Quake, Doom and
Half-Life maps and models to a view the way they do to a window.
